Feature: Forward flight with self-levelling
  FBWA is the self-levelling mode of the VTOL, and button 3 of the radio selects it. In FBWA,
  the sticks set the angle of bank and pitch. When you release the sticks, the plane levels
  its wings and nose by itself. Thus you can learn to fly the plane on its wing, with no
  risk of a roll-over. From a hover, FBWA also starts the transition: the wing motors tilt
  forward and the plane speeds up. In FBWA the throttle stick sets the power, so push it all the way up. At cruise speed, the wing needs a little nose up to hold the height: pull the elevator stick back a little.

  Expectation: FBWA changes a hover into forward flight
    Given the VTOL is hovering over the field
    When the pilot switches to forward flight
    Then the flight is cruising
    And the autopilot mode is FBWA
    And the height above home is at least 15 m

  Expectation: In FBWA, the aileron stick banks the plane
    Given the VTOL is cruising in FBWA
    When the aileron stick is right
    Then the VTOL's wings are banked

  Expectation: In FBWA, the wings become level again
    Release the stick, and the plane levels its wings by itself.
    Given the VTOL is cruising in FBWA
    And the aileron stick is right
    And the VTOL's wings are banked
    When the aileron stick is centred
    Then the VTOL's wings are level

  Expectation: Button 2 brings the plane out of FBWA and back to a hover
    Given the VTOL is cruising in FBWA
    When the pilot switches back to a hover
    Then the flight is hovering
    And the autopilot mode is QLOITER
    And the height above home is at least 15 m
